  [38;5;153mprefs.js[39m sits at the repo root next to [38;5;153mapp.js[39m (it's browser code; [38;5;153msrc/[39m is the unrelated Node stub and stays
  untouched). [38;5;153mindex.html[39m gains one [38;5;153m<script src="prefs.js"></script>[39m before [38;5;153mapp.js[39m, so [38;5;153mPrefs[39m exists by the time [38;5;153mapp.js[39m
  runs. No build step, no bundler, no module system — that matches what's there.

  The one wrinkle worth designing rather than stumbling into: a plain global reading [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m directly is untestable
  outside a browser. So the file defines a factory and a default instance:

  [34mfunction[39m [33mcreatePrefs[39m(storage) { [32m/* ... */[39m }
  [34mconst[39m [34mPrefs[39m = [33mcreatePrefs[39m(globalThis.localStorage);

  with a CommonJS export guarded by [38;5;153mtypeof module !== "undefined"[39m at the bottom. In the browser that line is inert and
  [38;5;153mPrefs[39m is a global, exactly like [38;5;153mlogin[39m and [38;5;153mvalidateForm[39m are today. Under Node, tests [38;5;153mrequire[39m the file and call
  [38;5;153mcreatePrefs(fakeStorage)[39m with a ~10-line in-memory stand-in. That's the whole testability story: no jsdom, no
  dependencies, and the browser path stays as dumb as the rest of the page.

  Alongside that, the tooling question — this repo has no [38;5;153mscripts[39m block, no test runner, no linter, nothing. Setting
  tooling up is never cheaper than right now, before there's code to retrofit:
